<!--
    kuna issue `kuna-condstmts` (P8, sub-stage goto-quality).

    ruleBlockOr folds a condition block into a `&&`/`||` operand when
    BlockBasic::isComplex calls it simple.  That verdict is taken before
    variables are merged, so a block whose loads later become explicit prints
    as a long comma chain inside the `if`.

    gcc -O1 x86-64 of:

      int f(int *a, unsigned char *k)
      { if (a[0] == 3 && a[6] == 10) {
          unsigned char b0 = k[0], b1 = k[1], b2 = k[2], b3 = k[3];
          if (check(a) == (b0 | b1 << 8 | b2 << 16 | (unsigned)b3 << 24)) {
            a[8] = 1; say(1); return 1; } }
        a[8] = 0; say(0); return 2; }

    The four byte loads sit in callee-saved registers across the call to
    `check`, so all four print as statements.

    Pass 1 (default, option condstmts 3): the block printing four statements
    before its test is not folded; the loads are statements inside the first
    `if` -> assertions #1, #2.
    Pass 2 (option condstmts off): the fold stands and the loads print as a
    five-element comma chain in the condition -> assertion #3.
-->
